## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of order statistics in dynamic sets, along with practical implementation details like lazy deletion and handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- **Median of a dynamic set**: the middle element (or average of two middle elements) when sorted
- **Two-heap approach**: max-heap for the lower half, min-heap for the upper half
- **Lazy deletion**: defer actual removal until top operations, tracking deletions via a count map
- **Duplicate handling**: maintain frequency counts rather than storing duplicate values multiple times in the heaps

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianFinder {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();    // max-heap for lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap();   // min-heap for upper half
    this.delayed = new Map();    // lazy deletion map
  }

  insert(num) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(num);
    } else {
      this.high.push(num);
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  rebalance() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  erase(num) {
    this.delayed.set(num, (this.delayed.get(num) || 0) + 1);
    if (num <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.deleted++;
    } else {
      this.high.deleted++;
    }
    this.rebalance();
  }

  getMedian() {
    this.prune(this.low);
    this.prune(this.high);
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    }
    return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a scalable data structure for tracking peak occupancy across a large domain with many overlapping intervals—a common problem in campaign management, resource allocation, and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Implicit segment tree**: Enables efficient operations over a massive coordinate space (up to 2^31-1) without building a full tree
- **Range add, range max with argmax**: Combines lazy propagation for updates with maintaining both value and position of the maximum
- **Incremental updates**: Insert and erase operations dynamically modify the tree state
- **Handling duplicates**: Multiple intervals covering the same coordinate naturally accumulate via additive weights

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.max = 0;
    this.argMax = 0;
    this.lazy = 0;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function rangeAdd(node, L, R, ql, qr, delta) {
  if (qr < L || R < ql) return;
  if (ql <= L && R <= qr) {
    node.max += delta;
    node.lazy += delta;
    return;
  }
  push(node);
  const mid = (L + R) >>> 1;
  rangeAdd(node.left, L, mid, ql, qr, delta);
  rangeAdd(node.right, mid + 1, R, ql, qr, delta);
  pull(node);
}
```

